<h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in Our World – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Purple Flower.</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify pictures that show what happened in a simple story heard. Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can listen to a simple story and identify corresponding pictures that illustrate key events (e.g., recognizing the picture of a blue bird mentioned in the story). Can identify simple words for very common symbols, matching them to images (e.g., linking "flower" with its picture). Can identify the initial sounds of letters of the alphabet in common objects and things (e.g., /f/ in "fish," /b/ in "bird," and /k/ in "cat").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 8px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #e0e0e0;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing large, colorful flashcards of objects and animals from the previous lesson (e.g., a blue bird, a purple flower, an orange cat, a green tree, a red house, a yellow leaf, blue sky). The teacher holds up each flashcard and asks, "What is this?" or "What color is it?" Students respond chorally or individually with simple words like "bird," "blue," "flower," "purple." The teacher then plays a short, simple song about colors and objects (e.g., "I See Colors Everywhere") and encourages students to point to objects of those colors in the classroom or on the flashcards, reinforcing vocabulary and linking to the theme "That's a Purple Flower."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new flashcards that show a word and its corresponding picture (e.g., a flashcard with the word "bird" and a picture of a bird; another with "flower" and a flower). The teacher holds up the flashcard, points to the word and says, "This is 'bird'." Then points to the picture and says, "This is a bird." The teacher models identifying the initial sound of key words. For example, "B-b-bird. /b/." "F-f-flower. /f/." "C-c-cat. /k/." The teacher demonstrates matching a word card (e.g., "blue") to a picture card (e.g., a blue bird), saying "This is a blue bird." The teacher models reading simple phrases slowly and clearly, connecting the words to the visual cues.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a flashcard with the word "flower" and its picture) "Is this a bird?" (Expected answer: "No, flower.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a red flower flashcard) "What color is this flower?" (Expected answer: "Red.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated storybook or large flashcards forming a sequence, titled "The Colors in Our Garden." The story features simple sentences like: "I see a blue bird. The bird is small. It flies in the sky. Look! That's a purple flower. It is a big flower. I see a green tree. The tree is tall. An orange cat sits under the tree. This is a red house. A yellow leaf is on the tree." Each sentence is accompanied by a clear, large picture. The story is presented as a narrative excerpt, suitable for 1st graders.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify pictures that show what happened in a simple story heard. Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Teacher asks, students point to picture):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the blue bird?" (Students point to the picture of the bird).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the flower?" (Students point to the purple flower).</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Teacher reads a statement, students show thumbs up/down for true/false based on pictures):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The cat is blue." (Teacher points to the orange cat picture. Students show thumbs down).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"That is a green tree." (Teacher points to the green tree picture. Students show thumbs up).</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Teacher holds up a word card, students find the matching picture in the story):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up "house" word card. Students point to the picture of the house.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up "leaf" word card. Students point to the picture of the leaf.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying simple words for very common symbols; identifying pictures from a story; phonemic awareness (/f/, /b/, /k/, /l/); Grammar: Color + noun, Present simple; Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red, tall, small, see, look, fly, sit. Pragmatic: Recognizing colors and objects, describing with colors.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Picture & Word Match-Up" worksheet.</b> Students receive a worksheet with a column of pictures (e.g., a blue bird, a purple flower, an orange cat, a green tree) and a column of corresponding words (e.g., "blue bird", "purple flower", "orange cat", "green tree"). Students draw a line to match each picture to its correct word/phrase. The teacher circulates, offering help with word recognition and prompting, e.g., "What color is this?" and "What is this?" to guide them to the correct word. The teacher also asks students to say the initial sound of words like "bird" (/b/) or "flower" (/f/) as they match.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Circle the Story Picture" activity.</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with small excerpts from the story. Each excerpt has one simple sentence (e.g., "I see a blue bird.") and three pictures below it (e.g., a blue bird, a red cat, a green tree). Students read (or listen to the teacher read) the sentence and circle the picture that matches the sentence. This activity reinforces identifying pictures that show what happened in a simple story and identifying simple words. The teacher emphasizes words with /l/ sound like "blue" as students circle.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Color & Count!" with Picture Cards.</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (e.g., three blue birds, two purple flowers, one orange cat). One student picks a card and reads the object (e.g., "bird"). The other student counts the objects of that color and says, "Three blue birds." They swap roles. This activity practices counting and identifying colors of objects, and reinforces "Color + noun" structure. The teacher monitors for correct identification and simple word reading, and encourages students to check each other's reading of the words.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Our Colorful World Book" Reading Identification.</b> Students work individually or in small groups (2-3 students). Each student/group receives a blank "book" (a few folded pages) with spaces for drawings and simple sentences. The teacher provides a set of pre-printed sentences or simple words related to colors and objects (e.g., "A red flower.", "Two green trees.", "An orange cat.", "A blue sky."). Students are tasked with selecting one or two sentences/words, reading them, and then drawing a picture that clearly illustrates what they read. For example, if a student picks "A purple flower," they draw a purple flower. If they pick "An orange cat," they draw an orange cat. This task directly connects to the theme "That's a Purple Flower." and scenario "Colors in Our World" by requiring students to identify words/phrases and represent their meaning visually. The focus is on correctly identifying the meaning of the written words through their drawing. They then "read" their drawing by pointing to it and saying the corresponding phrase or word.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students during the "Our Colorful World Book" activity. The checklist focuses on their ability to correctly identify and illustrate the meaning of the written words/phrases. For phonemic awareness, the teacher listens to students as they say the words for their drawings, noting if they produce the initial sounds /f/, /b/, /k/, and /l/ correctly.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Our Colorful World Book" pages to the teacher or a small group. They point to their drawing and say the sentence or word they illustrated (e.g., pointing to a drawing of a red flower and saying "A red flower"). The teacher assesses their ability to match the picture to the word/phrase they were given and their simple oral production.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of words/phrases (picture accurately reflects the text).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., "flower," "bird," "blue," "purple").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of initial sounds (e.g., /f/ in "flower," /b/ in "bird," /k/ in "cat," /l/ in "leaf" and "blue") when presenting.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the drawing in representing the text.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "happy face" if they understood the words and could draw them, or a "neutral face" if they needed help. The teacher can also ask: "Can you read 'purple flower'?" (Students point to the words/picture or give a thumbs up/down.)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about colors did you read today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture to read and draw?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson for 1st graders? (e.g., the visual support, matching activities, the storybook).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to identify words or pictures, or with initial sounds? (e.g., more 1-on-1 sound practice, simpler words, larger print).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better integrate phonemic awareness for /l/ and other sounds in future reading activities?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson helped students identify words and relate them to pictures and sounds. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact using these identified words and colors, for example, by describing what they see in pictures to their peers, practicing "What color is it?" and "It is a [color] [noun]."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>